WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
The Lambda execution role grants the function permission to access AWS services and resources.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.320
The code running inside the function receives the effective permissions assigned to that role.

3
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:19.100
If the role can read a table, write to a queue, publish a message, or call another service, then the function code can attempt that action.

4
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:26.420
Least privilege means scoping permissions to the minimum actions, resources, and conditions required for the workload.

5
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.240
A function that reads one table should not receive broad write access to many services.

6
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:37.020
A function that sends one type of message should not receive permission to manage unrelated infrastructure.

7
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.100
Avoid wildcard permissions for convenience.

8
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:48.200
Broad actions and broad resources make it harder to reason about what a function can do after a code change, configuration mistake, or dependency issue.

9
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:55.660
Separate roles by workload, environment, tenant boundary, or privilege level when that makes the permission model clearer.

10
00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:02.180
Resource-based policies control who or what can invoke Lambda resources in supported scenarios.

11
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:11.960
Cross-account access, service invocation, layers, aliases, versions, and function URLs all require explicit policy review.

12
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.940
Invocation permission and execution permission are related, but they answer different questions.

13
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:25.860
Administrative, data export, write, delete, payment, configuration, and high-cost operations need extra review.

14
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:35.080
A secure role is narrow enough that reviewers can explain why each permission exists and what production signal would show if that permission is being used unexpectedly.
